<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
Do not double-click this file: Finder opens it in Xcode. Install it with launchctl.

cp com.erezvolk.flipkeys.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.erezvolk.flipkeys 2>/dev/null
launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.erezvolk.flipkeys.plist
launchctl kickstart gui/$(id -u)/com.erezvolk.flipkeys

The --matching predicate limits the flip to the built-in keyboard and to Apple
Magic Keyboards. Any other external keyboard keeps its own layout.

hidutil matches a service by Product, VendorID, or ProductID. It ignores
UsagePage and Usage. To get the Product string of a keyboard, run:

hidutil list
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.erezvolk.flipkeys</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/bin/hidutil</string>
        <string>property</string>
        <string>--matching</string>
        <string>[{"Product":"Apple Internal Keyboard / Trackpad"},{"Product":"Magic Keyboard"},{"Product":"Magic Keyboard with Numeric Keypad"},{"Product":"Magic Keyboard with Touch ID"},{"Product":"Magic Keyboard with Touch ID and Numeric Keypad"}]</string>
        <string>--set</string>
        <string>{"UserKeyMapping":[{"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingSrc":0x700000035,"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingDst":0x700000064},{"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingSrc":0x700000064,"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingDst":0x700000035}]}</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
